#pragma once

/**
 * @file features.h
 * @brief How this copy of librats was built: which optional modules it contains,
 *        and whether it is a DLL.
 *
 * Generated by CMake from features.h.in — do not edit the generated copy.
 *
 * All three macros are build-time switches that change the *installed headers*,
 * not just which .cpp files compile, so a consumer reading the headers with one
 * of them in a different state than the library was built with sees something
 * other than what it links against:
 *
 *   - RATS_SEARCH_FEATURES — dht/dht.h, dht/node.h and subsystems/bittorrent.h
 *     declare the spider API behind it, and dht::Node declares *data members*
 *     behind it. Getting it wrong is a missing declaration at best and a silently
 *     different object layout at worst.
 *   - RATS_STORAGE — same mechanism; no installed header tests it today.
 *   - RATS_IMPORT_DLL — MSVC only: without it RATS_API expands to nothing, so a
 *     consumer of the shared build compiles as if the library were static and
 *     loses __declspec(dllimport) on every exported symbol (see rats_export.h).
 *     Not set for a static build, where the same define would send the consumer
 *     looking for __imp_-prefixed symbols that do not exist. RATS_EXPORT_DLL is
 *     deliberately absent: it is PRIVATE to the library's own translation units,
 *     and rats_export.h tests it first, so it still wins there.
 *
 * CMakeLists.txt also sets these on the `rats` target (PUBLIC for the features,
 * INTERFACE for RATS_IMPORT_DLL), so a consumer using find_package(rats CONFIG) +
 * rats::rats gets them via INTERFACE_COMPILE_DEFINITIONS. That path alone is not
 * enough: vcpkg's MSBuild integration, a hand-rolled -I<prefix>/include build, and
 * IDE indexers never read the CMake target. Recording the answers in an installed
 * header makes the headers self-describing for every consumer, whatever their
 * build system, and removes the need for Conan's package_info() to repeat them.
 *
 * Included from rats_export.h, which practically every public header pulls in,
 * so the macros are set before any #ifdef that tests them. The #ifndef guards
 * keep the definition benign when the compile definition already supplied it.
 */

#ifndef RATS_SEARCH_FEATURES
#cmakedefine RATS_SEARCH_FEATURES
#endif

#ifndef RATS_STORAGE
#cmakedefine RATS_STORAGE
#endif

#ifndef RATS_IMPORT_DLL
#cmakedefine RATS_IMPORT_DLL
#endif
